    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d797">

                <pb facs="1007" n="908" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg908"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.2331/53: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dearing</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-01-14T11:00:00-05:00">January 14, 1936—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4. Referring to the Embassy’s telegram 2, January 9, 2 p.m.,<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d797-f1"/> Minister of Finance decree of January 8th, suspends
                    higher import duties on woolen and cotton textiles from Great Britain until
                    negotiations for Commercial Treaty with England has been approved.</p>
                <pb facs="1008" n="909" xml:id="pg909"/>
                <p>The decree mentions that the treaty negotiations almost concluded and that action
                    taken is intended to prevent obstacles to favorable termination of this
                    agreement.</p>
                <p>This decree, which the Minister of Finance has ordered not to be made public, is
                    discriminatory and gives competitive advantages to British goods.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dearing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
